# controllermap manifest — which USB controller gets which Engine mapping.
#
# Format, whitespace separated:
#   <vid:pid>   <mapping-dir>   [description]
#
# <vid:pid>      lowercase hex as it appears in sysfs, e.g. 154e:1000.
#                Run `controllermap.sh --list` with the controller plugged in
#                to print the ids actually present.
# <mapping-dir>  a directory under mappings/ containing a single file,
#                RMZ2_Controller_Assignments.qml, written against that
#                controller's own notes/CCs.
#
# Authoring a mapping: copy the vendor RMZ2_Controller_Assignments.qml as a
# starting point and change the note/CC numbers to what your controller emits
# (`aseqdump`-style capture, or the controller's MIDI implementation chart).
# The structure — which QML component provides which function — stays as-is;
# only the numbers and the per-section midiChannel need to change. See
# docs/ENGINEOS.md for the RMZ2 defaults (decks on MIDI channels 0x04/0x05,
# mixer channels 0x00/0x01, global 0x0F).
#
# Nothing is listed by default: a mapping's note/CC numbers can be written from
# a MIDI implementation chart, but the vid:pid can only be read off the unit,
# so every line here has to be filled in with the hardware in hand. With no
# match, the vendor RMZ2 mapping stays installed, which is what
# midisurface drives on its own.
#
# mappings/denon-mc6000mk2 is written and ready to use — it only lacks the
# product id below. See its README.md for what it maps and which of its
# assumptions to verify first.
#
# Examples (commented — vendor ids are real, product ids are placeholders and
# must be confirmed against your unit with --list before use):
#
154e:5021   denon-mc6000mk2      Denon DJ MC6000MK2
